$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Typical experimental procedure for the epoxidation reactions described in
Table 1, Table 3 and Scheme 10: The reaction was carried out in a flat
bottomed glass vial (diameter 15mm) equipped with a magnetic stirrer
flea. In the vial, were placed the amine 1 or the hydrochloride salt of
amine 1, 2, 3, 7 or 8 (5 or 10 mol %, 0.0212 or 0.042 mmol), and
1-phenylcyclohexene (67 mg, 0.424 mmol). The mixture was dissolved in
CH3CN/H2O (0.5 mL) and pyridine (0.5 eq., 0.212 mmol, 17 μL) was added
followed by finely ground NaHCO3 (356 mg, 4.24 mmol) and Oxone® (521 mg,
0.848 mmol). The reaction was stirred at room temperature or at -10°C and
followed by TLC. If the reaction was not complete, stirring was continued
for a maximum of 2 hours. Salts were removed by filtration with a sinter
over MgSO4. The cake was washed with CH2Cl2 (25 mL). When the reaction
was performed at -10°C (Table 1, entries 4, 7 and 9), the organic phase
was extracted with a saturated solution of NaHCO3 (to recover the amine).
After evaporation of the dried organic phase, the crude mixture was
analyzed by 1H NMR. Yields and conversions are determined by 1H NMR
relative to an internal standard (para-dimethoxybenzene). The
enantioselectivities were determined by chiral GC using a α-CD column.
(S,S)-1-phenylcyclohexene oxide[4], yield 99% 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3, 25
°C): = 1.20-1.71 (m, 4H; CH2), 1.92-2.38 (m, 4H; CH2), 3.01 (m, 1H; CHO),
7.21-7.53 (m, 5H; aromatic H). The absolute configuration was determined
by comparing the measured optical rotation with the reported one: [α]D20
= -54.8 (c = 0.47 in toluene) [lit.[5] for the
(R,R)-1-phenylcyclohex-1-ene oxide: [α]D25 = +116.7 (c = 1.21 in benzene)
for 98% ee].
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM New Insights in the Mechanism of Amine Catalyzed Epoxidation: Dual Role
of Protonated Ammonium Salts as Both Phase Transfer Catalysts and
Activators of Oxone
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Aggarwal, Varinder K.; Lopin, Chrystel; Sandrinelli, Franck
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2003), 125(25), 7596-7601

