$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Procedure for the Epoxidation of the Enecarbamates 1 with
Dimethyldioxirane (GP1) The enecarbamate 1 (1.0 equiv.) was dissolved in
acetone (30 mL) and 0.50 g of molecular sieves (5 Å) was added under an
argon-gas atmosphere. The solution was cooled to 0 °C and after 10 min
magnetic stirring, 1.0 equiv. of dimethyldioxirane (0.09 M in acetone)
was added. The reaction mixture was allowed to warm up to ca. 20 °C while
stirring, and the reaction progress was monitored for the presence of DMD
by iodometry (KI/HOAC). After complete DMD consumption (the reaction
times are given for the individual cases), the reaction mixture was
centrifuged to remove the molecular sieves, the sieves were washed with
acetone (2x15 mL) and centrifuged again. From the combined centrifugates,
the acetone was removed (20 °C, 30 mbar) and the epoxide 2 was obtained
as only product. The epoxides 2 were too labile for purification by
silica-gel chromatography, and were characterized by their 1H-and 13C-NMR
spectra and exact mass determination on the diastereomeric mixture
[except epoxide 2a, which decomposed completely already at room
temperature (ca. 20 °C) in a couple of hours]. The diastereomeric ratio
was determined by 1H-NMR analysis directly on the crude reaction mixture,
through integration of the areas under the characteristic NMR signals. In
the case of nearly 1:1 mixtures of the diastereomers, COSY, HMBC and HMQC
experiments were carried out for rigorous assignment. For the
configurational assignment of the individual diastereomers, see General
Procedure GP3. The epoxides 2b-d persist as neat solids for several
months at -20 °C, but they readily decompose in solution in the presence
of acid traces at room temperature (ca. 20 °C). For this reason, after
formation, the labile epoxides must be immediately worked up to remove
the solvent and the molecular sieves to minimize decomposition.
Epoxidation of the Enecarbamate Z-1a by DMD According to the general
procedure GP1, 397.5 mg (1.96 mmol) of enecarbamate Z-1a were epoxidized
with DMD during 1 h. After work-up, the racemic epoxide (1S*,2S*)-2a was
obtained as colorless oil in 94% yield (403.8 mg). The material
decomposed at ca. 20 °C within a couple of hours, such that an exact-mass
determination was not possible. Characterisation rests only on the
NMR-spectral data.
(1S*,2S*)-3-(3-Methyl-3-phenyloxyranyl)oxazolidin-2-one [(1S*,2S*)-2a].
1H-NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ 1.64 (s, 3 H), 2.27-2.39 (m, 1 H), 3.05-3.16
(m, 1 H), 3.89-4.00 (m, 1 H), 4.04-4.14 (m, 1 H), 4.97 (s, 1 H),
7.18-7.56 (m, 5 H); 13C-NMR (151 MHz, CDCl3) δ 24.4 (q), 41.6 (d), 62.6
(t), 63.4 (s), 69.6 (d), 126.4 (d), 128.1 (d), 128.5 (d), 137.8 (s),
158.4 (s).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM mol. sieves added stage 1
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Chiral-Auxiliary-Controlled Diastereoselectivity in the Epoxidation of
Enecarbamates with DMD and mCPBA
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Adam, Waldemar; Bosio, Sara G.; Wolff, Barbara T.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2003), 5(6), 819-822

